// Real data transcribed directly from Brody Haar's own Canyon Framework
// marathon training plan, as he provided it -- every dayLabel,
// plannedDistanceM, RPE, and pace-percent value below is exactly as
// authored. Nothing here is invented (see CLAUDE.md §5/§15 and this
// feature's own plan doc). Two source documents together make up "as he
// provided it": a compact day-by-day table (mileage/RPE/a short per-day
// prescription) AND a separate "Canyon Overview.pdf" attachment with
// richer per-week narrative coaching commentary -- both are captured here;
// the first pass at this seed only used the table and missed the PDF
// entirely, which is what CANYON_OVERVIEW/CANYON_WEEK_NOTES and each day's
// appended paragraph (below the original short table note) now fix.
//
// His original day-by-day table is expressed here as one CanyonDayInput
// per day, split into the three real phases he described -- General (10
// weeks), Supportive (4 weeks), Specific (4 weeks) -- keyed by phase-
// relative week number (1-indexed) and day of week (1=Monday..7=Sunday),
// matching program_days' own week_number/day_of_week keying exactly.
//
// pacePercentBasis/Min/Max is populated ONLY where the day's whole
// prescription is a single, unambiguous "NN[-NN]% 5k" or "NN[-NN]% MP"
// clause (e.g. "4 mi at 85% 5k", "15-16 mi at 90-92% MP") -- a genuine
// multi-segment progression (several distinct "at NN%" clauses, or a
// compound "98-100//100//..." target) is deliberately left unstructured
// at this level; its exact wording is preserved only in `notes`, never
// approximated by picking one of several segments.
//
// Each day's `notes` field holds the original short table prescription
// FIRST, then (for the ~45 days the PDF specifically discusses) a blank
// line followed by the PDF's own paragraph about that exact workout --
// cross-checked against the table's own numbers (rep counts, percentages,
// distances) before being attached, e.g. "Wednesday's 1k repeats... up
// from 500m last week" was only attached to the day whose table row is
// literally "7 x 1 km at 95% 5k" following a prior week's "500m" session.
// A subsection of the PDF that isn't tied to one specific day (a whole-
// week framing note, an end-of-week fitness-estimate-review procedure, the
// closing taper paragraph) becomes that week's entry in CANYON_WEEK_NOTES
// instead -- authoring/reference commentary for the coach
// (program_phase_weeks.notes), never generated into an athlete's real
// schedule (see that column's own migration comment).
//
// CANYON_OVERVIEW is the PDF's own page 1 (framework overview) and page 2
// (glossary), used as this Program's own description.
//
// A "12-Week" duration variant was deliberately NOT created here: Brody's
// message described using both 12- and 18-week versions of this
// methodology, but only this 18-week day-by-day table (and its matching
// 18-week overview PDF) was actually provided. Inventing a distinct,
// shorter General phase (or guessing which of these 10 real weeks would
// make a good 12-week compression) would be fabricating training content
// this file's whole purpose is to avoid. General's phase definition (see
// seed-canyon-program.ts) is nonetheless modeled as a real min/max WEEK
// RANGE, not a fixed count, so a coach can already build a shorter variant
// later that reuses these same real weeks (selecting fewer of them at
// assignment time) or author a genuinely different General phase for it,
// without any schema change.
//
// The optional Base Phase Brody mentioned (used "when the athlete has
// additional time") is created as a real, is_optional phase definition
// with a sensible duration range and zero authored days -- exactly as the
// implementation plan's own §6 directs, since no Base Phase content was
// ever provided either.

export type CanyonDayInput = {
  weekNumber: number;
  dayOfWeek: number; // 1=Mon..7=Sun
  dayLabel: string;
  plannedDistanceM: number | null;
  plannedRpe: number | null;
  plannedRpeMax: number | null;
  pacePercentBasis: "5k" | "marathon" | null;
  pacePercentMin: number | null;
  pacePercentMax: number | null;
  notes: string;
};

// The Program's own overview -- shared across every duration variant under
// it (see training_programs' own overview migration comment: a 12-week and
// an 18-week version of the same methodology have ONE overview, not one
// per variant). Split into the structured fields the overview UI actually
// renders, rather than one undifferentiated text blob.
export const CANYON_DESCRIPTION = `The Canyon framework is a good introduction to real marathon training for runners with little or no background in serious training. To be sure, 40-50 miles per week is not enough to reach your potential in the marathon and you won't be as well-prepared for the last six miles of the race as you would with a higher mileage plan. Still, for newer marathon runners, that's a worthwhile trade for a manageable mileage level and a low-risk way to learn the ropes of marathon training and racing.
Talented runners will be able to break 3:00 with this training plan, but most will be running in the 3:15-4:30 range. For many marathoners, completing the Canyon framework will be an early step on their journey as a marathoner.
The Canyon framework's greatest strengths are its modest time requirements, less challenging workouts, and lower injury risk. Canyon is able to sidestep some of the downsides of low weekly mileage by including two to three-day blocks of higher volume-averaging, for example, 10 mi/day for three days in a row, which gives you some of the benefits of a 70-mile week. These mini-blocks of volume start to prepare your body for higher mileage and longer workouts later in your marathon career.`;

