export const CANYON_WEEK_NOTES: { phaseKey: "General" | "Supportive" | "Specific"; weekNumber: number; notes: string }[] = [
  { phaseKey: "General", weekNumber: 1, notes: "Make sure you keep the pace easy on both this week and next week's long easy run. These are \"time on feet\" (TOF) runs -> any fatigue at the end should be solely from duration, not intensity." },
  { phaseKey: "General", weekNumber: 2, notes: "EOW\n- Do a quick review of your workouts over the last few weeks. Worth checking to make sure your 5k pace isn't way too fast or way too slow.\n- If you're able to hit your paces in workouts and your perceived difficulty is close to the target difficulty levels, great: continue with your current fitness estimate as-is.\n- If not, you'll want to adjust your current fitness estimate up or down accordingly." },
  { phaseKey: "General", weekNumber: 4, notes: "First \"down week\" - reduction in mileage and only two workouts. Use this week as an opportunity to freshen up and consolidate your fitness gains.\n\nEOW\nRevise your current fitness estimate. Your recent workouts at 85, 90, and 100% 5k pace are a good guide, but another trick you can employ is reviewing the fastest mile of your Kenyan-style progression runs -- typically, runners at your mileage level can close out a progression run with the last mile at 90-92% of their current 5k pace." },
  { phaseKey: "General", weekNumber: 7, notes: "First Peak Mileage Week (Congrats!!)\nDon't be surprised if you have some fatigue and soreness this week. Even if that fatigue affects some of your workouts, it's no cause for alarm. In the general phase, we're aiming for a high overall volume (training load), not top-quality individual workouts. As long as the effort is correct, you're moving in the right direction." },
  { phaseKey: "General", weekNumber: 8, notes: "Down week\nThe down \"week\" really starts after Tuesday. For the remainder of the week, runs are shorter and easier, and you have no other real workouts. Coming off the tough session on Tuesday, use the lower volume and lack of a second midweek workout to regenerate and freshen up. If you feel good and are really itching for more quality, you can turn Friday into a Kenyan-style progression run, but in most cases, you should stick with an easy to moderate run.\n\nEOW\nPaces from Tuesday's interval workout will be helpful when revising your current fitness estimate, but make sure you also pay attention to how your workouts went during Week 6 and Week 7." },
  { phaseKey: "Supportive", weekNumber: 1, notes: "Converting 5K fitness estimate into a MP estimate\nTwo especially helpful workouts for narrowing in on marathon pace are last week's strong run (7 mi at 85% 5k) and Week 7's long fast run (11-12 mi at 75-80% 5k). Compare how you felt on these runs to get a sense for whether your current marathon pace is closer to 80% or 85% of your current 5k pace -- 81% 5k pace is a good default, but you can bump that up or down a few percentage points based on how your training has been going. Don't worry about running 100% MP for a full 26 miles just yet: think of it instead as the fastest pace you could sustain for ~15-16 miles in a really tough workout." },
  { phaseKey: "Supportive", weekNumber: 2, notes: "More emphasis on recovering well after big workouts\nInstead of a continually high training load, as was our goal in the general phase, we're shifting to bigger workouts with more recovery afterwards (a welcome break after a few weeks of tough, consistent training)." },
  { phaseKey: "Specific", weekNumber: 1, notes: "Marathon-specific phase has arrived\nOur attention is even more focused on running well in the marathon-specific sessions on the weekend and recovering well afterwards. Midweek workouts will be less intense, and the days following big workouts will be very easy.\n\nEOW\nUpdate your fitness estimate one more time. At this point, your current fitness estimate will be, at least provisionally, GMP. You can make small adjustments as needed in the last few weeks, but you should think about your workouts from here on out as based off the pace you'll target on race day." },
  { phaseKey: "Specific", weekNumber: 2, notes: "After last week, there's a good chance you'll be sore and fatigued for a few days\nKeep the effort easy on your easy runs, and remember that it's normal for your easy pace to be significantly slower for a few days as you recover from a big workout." },
  { phaseKey: "Specific", weekNumber: 3, notes: "Allowing a wider range in mileage\nHelps ensure that you're able to recover after last weekend's marathon-specific session. Keep your easy days easy, and make sure you're getting enough sleep." },
  { phaseKey: "Specific", weekNumber: 4, notes: "Training during race week is very simple\nNothing new, just one light workout and a few shorter easy runs to make sure you arrive at the starting line feeling good.\n\nI generally recommend a taper that sticks to the higher end of the mileage range for the runs leading up to race day, with the intent of treating race day just like it's a very tough marathon-specific workout. If you prefer a more aggressive taper that decreases mileage to a greater extent, you can stick to the shorter end of the mileage ranges for this week's easy runs instead." },
];